<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Australian and New Zealand Army Corps</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1914">1914</a>–<a href="/years/1916">1916</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as ANZAC</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Australian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> Army Corps was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> army corps formed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in December 1914 under the British general William Birdwood. Its landing at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Gallipoli</a> on 25 April 1915 and the costly campaign that followed made the 'Anzac' tradition a foundation of national identity in both countries. The corps was reorganised in 1916 for service on the Western Front.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/australian-and-new-zealand-army-corps.jpg" alt="Australian and New Zealand Army Corps" width="320" height="206" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: GrahamBould · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anzac_Cove_encampment_1915.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Plans for the corps began in November 1914, while the first Australian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> contingents were still at sea, bound, as they thought, for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Because the Canadian Expeditionary Force on Salisbury Plain had suffered shortages of accommodation and equipment, it was decided to spare the Australians and New Zealanders the English winter and send them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> for training. Horatio Kitchener, the British Secretary of State for War, appointed Lieutenant General William Birdwood of the British Indian Army to command, and Birdwood took most of the corps staff from the Indian Army. He arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> on 21 December 1914.</p><p>The corps was first to be named the Australasian Army Corps, but complaints from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> recruits led to the name Australian and New Zealand Army Corps. Clerks found it too long and used the abbreviation A. &amp; N.Z.A.C., then ANZAC, which was adopted as a code name but did not enter common use among the troops until after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Gallipoli</a> landings.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>At the outset the corps had two divisions: the Australian Division and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> and Australian Division. Though linked with the two countries, it was multinational, and at various points included British officers, Indian units and the Zion Mule Corps.</p><p>After the evacuation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Gallipoli</a> in December 1915 the troops reassembled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. The New Zealanders formed their own New Zealand Division, and the Australian force was reorganised into new divisions. They formed I ANZAC Corps under Birdwood, which left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in early 1916, and II ANZAC Corps under Lieutenant General Alexander Godley. The ANZAC Mounted Division was formed in March 1916 for Egypt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>. The corps was briefly re-established as ANZAC Corps on 12 April 1941 under Lieutenant General Blamey during the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The word ANZAC came to name the soldiers themselves, first those at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Gallipoli</a> landings, then all Australian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> soldiers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> and now defence personnel more broadly. It also named the landing place, Anzac Cove, and survives in Anzac biscuits and ANZAC Day, which commemorates the landings and all who served or died in wars.</p><p>The term is protected in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> and the United Kingdom. In 1916 the United Kingdom prohibited its use in trade without government approval, cancelling about 29 trademarks, among them Anzac Motor Company and Anzac Soap. In 2003 the two countries registered it as an official sign under the Paris Convention.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Australian and New Zealand Army Corps” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The landing of the <b>Australian and New Zealand Army Corps</b> at Gallipoli on 25 April 1915, commemorated as Anzac Day, became a founding national legend, and the Australian Corps under Sir John Monash fought with distinction on the Western Front in 1918.</blockquote><a href="/empire/commonwealth-of-australia">Commonwealth of Australia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The tune lives on in &quot;My Bougainville&quot;, the anthem of the Autonomous Region of Bougainville, and Dawson's recording is heard in the television series <b>ANZAC</b> Girls.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-song-of-australia">The Song of Australia</a></li></ul></section>
